  "analysis": "Tabs vs. spaces is largely a matter of convention rather than technical superiority. Tabs: (1) allow each developer to set their own preferred indentation width via editor settings, (2) result in smaller file sizes (marginally, negligible with modern storage), (3) are semantically 'indentation' characters, matching intent. However, tabs render inconsistently across tools (diffs, terminals, code review platforms, print) unless everyone configures tab width identically, and mixing tabs/spaces (e.g., for alignment beyond indentation) causes visual bugs. Spaces: (1) render identically everywhere regardless of editor/terminal settings, (2) allow precise alignment of wrapped lines, arguments, or comments that tabs cannot reliably achieve, (3) are enforced consistently by most modern formatters (e.g., Prettier, Black, gofmt in spirit) reducing bikeshedding, (4) are the de facto default in many major style guides (Google, PEP8, Airbnb) and languages (Python's PEP8 mandates spaces; Go uses tabs via gofmt as a notable exception). The main practical costs of spaces are marginally larger diffs when indentation changes and no per-developer width customization. Most large-scale style guides and linters converge on spaces for consistency across heterogeneous tooling (CI logs, GitHub diffs, code review UIs), which tends to matter more in team/enterprise settings than individual editor customization. Given this is an internal multi-engineer codebase where consistency across viewing/review tools matters more than individual preference, spaces present fewer practical failure modes.",
